= Compiler — Simple =

A '''compiler''' translates a program from one representation into another.

For example, it can turn source code into machine code that a processor can run.

A compiler often:

* reads the program;
* checks its structure and meaning;
* builds an internal representation;
* improves or rearranges code;
* generates target code.

A compiler is not the same as an interpreter.

An interpreter runs code more directly, although real language systems can use both compiling and interpreting.

